// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// GOLD TREND — TRAILING STOP SYSTEM
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// Built 2026-07-31 from the first properly validated research this repo
// has produced: 157,366 bars of XAUUSD 15m (Dec 2019 - Jul 2026), split
// 60/40, parameters chosen on the training half ONLY and the held-out
// block evaluated once.
//
// OUT-OF-SAMPLE RESULT (2023-11-28 -> 2026-07-30, entry 50 / trail 8.0)
//     275 trades | WR 41.45% | PF 1.588 | +60.9% | max DD 5.5%
//     buy & hold over the same window: +100.4%, max DD 29.2%
//     return/drawdown: 11.07 vs 3.44  <- this is the actual edge
//
// ── THE CONFLUENCE STACK (added 2026-07-31) ─────────────────────────
// Four things now have to line up before a trade is taken, and a fifth
// decides where it is closed. Full period, 157,366 bars, each layer
// added on top of the one above it:
//
//   layer                          n     PF     net      maxDD   ret/DD
//   breakout + trail only        921   1.333  +152.5%   13.61%   11.21
//   + key-level exit (shorts)    922   1.379  +155.9%   12.30%   12.68
//   + slow SMA must agree        863   1.421  +157.7%    9.63%   16.36
//
// The last row is the shipped configuration. Note what the confluence
// actually bought: return barely moved (+155.9% -> +157.7%) but the
// drawdown fell by a fifth. It is a risk filter, not a return generator,
// and that is the honest way to describe every filter in this script.
//
// Both halves agree. Train PF 1.289 -> 1.335 with drawdown 12.3% -> 9.6%;
// out of sample 1.610 -> 1.624. Requiring only ONE of the two SMAs is
// much worse (train PF 1.149) -- their AGREEMENT is the signal, not
// either average by itself. Replacing SMA750 with SMA2000 rather than
// running both is also worse (train 1.181).
//
// The one confluence that did NOT pay: requiring a key level near the
// ENTRY. Full period net drops +157.7% -> +120.9%. It is a toggle in
// group 4, defaulted off, and the reason is in its tooltip.
//
// ── READ THIS BEFORE YOU TRADE IT ───────────────────────────────────
// THE ENTRY IS NOT AN EDGE. Random long entries pushed through this
// exact trailing exit scored a median PF of 1.621 on the same data;
// the Donchian breakout scored 1.588. The entry contributed -0.033.
// What makes money here is the WIDE TRAILING STOP holding a long
// position through a market that rose 179%, plus the stop capping the
// damage when it does not.
//
// So this is risk management on long gold exposure, not prediction. It
// is stated plainly because four months of this project was spent
// tuning entry logic that measurement says contributes nothing.
//
// WHY EVERY EARLIER ENGINE HERE LOST: they used fixed 1R/2R targets and
// 36-bar time stops. That caps winners at twice the stop while losers
// run to it, in a market whose entire return came from a handful of
// multi-month moves. Tested over the same 6.7 years that family scored
// PF 0.86 -- and PF 0.912 on the LONG side of a market that tripled.
// Stop width is the dominant variable, monotonically:
//     trail 2 ATR -> PF 1.05     trail 8 ATR -> PF 1.59
//     trail 4 ATR -> PF 1.40     trail 14 ATR -> PF 1.75
// and drawdown FALLS as the stop widens, because wide stops stop
// getting whipsawed.
//
// ── DIRECTION IS A REGIME BET, NOT A SETTING ────────────────────────
// An earlier version of this file claimed "long-only beat long/short on
// every setting tested". That was measured on the TRAINING half only,
// which was almost entirely a bull market. It was an overgeneralisation
// and the user found the counterexample immediately.
//
// Year by year (entry 50, trail 8 ATR):
//    year   gold    long-only PF   long+short PF
//    2020  +24.9%       2.212          1.752
//    2021   -4.2%       0.710          0.883     <- shorts win
//    2022   -0.4%       1.101          0.961
//    2023  +12.9%       1.189          0.962
//    2024  +27.0%       1.709          0.850
//    2025  +64.7%       1.625          1.009
//    2026   -5.8%       1.208          1.580     <- shorts win
//
// Long-only wins every RISING year. Long+short wins the FALLING ones.
// Over the full 6.7 years long-only is far ahead (PF 1.422, +155.6%,
// DD 14.2% vs PF 1.112, +51.6%, DD 23.8%) -- but that period was roughly
// 80% bull market, so the aggregate favours it by construction.
//
// Feb-Jul 2026, gold -15.8%:
//    long only    PF 0.567   -5.14%
//    long+short   PF 1.444   +6.94%   <- the current regime
//
// A regime filter does NOT resolve this automatically. An EMA gate was
// tested at 100/200/400/800/1600 and none recovered long-only's edge;
// the best (EMA 400) returned PF 1.110 over the full period, no better
// than plain long+short. So the switch stays MANUAL and explicit rather
// than hidden behind a filter that does not work.
//
// SHIPPING DEFAULT IS NOW LONG+SHORT, because gold has fallen over the
// last six months. If it resumes trending up, switch to long only.
//
// ── THE SMA TREND FILTER: WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT DO ──────────────
// Requested as SMA 2000. Swept 250-6000 on both halves. 2000 is NOT a
// good setting: it improves the TEST half and makes the TRAIN half
// worse, which is the signature of a regime artifact rather than a
// filter. Longer is monotonically better on the test half only, because
// that block is a sustained bull run.
//
// SMA 750 (~8 days) was selected on the TRAINING half alone and improves
// every window measured:
//    window            gold     no SMA        SMA 750
//    train 2019-23    +39.2%   PF 1.116     PF 1.202
//    test  2023-26   +100.4%   PF 1.025     PF 1.193
//    Feb-Jul 2026     -15.8%   PF 1.444     PF 1.911
//    full 6.7 years  +179.1%   PF 1.112     PF 1.206
//
// BUT IT FAILS ALL THREE SIGNIFICANCE GATES on the held-out block:
//    random entries with the SAME filter and exit: median PF 1.130,
//      95th pct 1.490. Ours 1.193 sits at the 60.7th percentile.  FAIL
//    deflated Sharpe 0.020 at a realistic trial count.               FAIL
//    return/drawdown 2.26 vs buy-and-hold 3.44.                      FAIL
//
// So: it lifts the headline numbers, it is not harmful, and it is NOT an
// edge. Ships ON because it improves every measured window, and is
// labelled honestly because believing it is an edge is how this project
// spent four months.
//
// Exiting on the SMA cross was also tested and REJECTED - it cuts
// winners early (train PF 1.030 vs 1.048). Off by default.
//
// ── QUARTERLY THEORY: WHAT WORKS AND WHAT DESTROYS IT ───────────────
// Both quarter theories in this repo were implemented and tested.
//
// YOTOV (price-based, 250-pt large quarters): REJECTED. Median ATR(14)
// on 15m gold is 2.58 points, so a 6-ATR trail sits ~15 points from
// price -- a 250-point quarter is 16x wider than the distance the system
// risks and is almost never reached. Used as a stop anchor it produced
// byte-identical results to the ATR trail alone: the constraint never
// bound once across 157,000 bars. Rescaling to 100/50/25-pt grids all
// landed at PF 1.08-1.09 against 1.172 with no target.
//
// DAYE (time-based) is the one that works, but ONLY asymmetrically.
// His daily quarters (ET): Q1 Asia 18-00 range build, Q2 London 00-06
// expansion, Q3 NY AM 06-12 continuation, Q4 NY PM 12-18 reversal and
// PROFIT-TAKING. Banking at the Q4 open, full period:
//    symmetric  25%  PF 1.100  +42.6%   <- worse than no take
//    symmetric  50%  PF 1.058  +21.4%   <- much worse
//    symmetric  75%  PF 1.010   +3.3%
//    symmetric full  PF 0.982   -7.3%
//    SHORTS ONLY 50% PF 1.132  +62.9%   <- better than no take, on
//                                          train AND test AND full
// Taking profit on the with-trend side removes exactly the position size
// that captures the large move. Taking it on the counter-trend side, where
// there is no large move to catch, is free. That asymmetry is the whole
// finding.
//
// ── JEAFX KEY LEVELS (the best improvement measured) ────────────────
// Separate source: quarter-round levels .00/.25/.50/.75, used as reversal
// areas. Scale translation is the whole point -- his EURUSD grid is 25
// pips against a ~10-pip ATR, and gold's 10-unit handles quartered give
// 2.50 points against a 2.58 ATR. Same ratio, faithful to the source.
// Banking 50% of the COUNTER-TREND leg at that grid, full period:
//    grid    PF      net       DD     return/DD
//    none   1.186   +87.0%   23.2%      3.75
//    2.50   1.261  +110.6%   19.0%      5.81   <- shipped
//    5.00   1.256  +108.7%   19.7%      5.52
//   25.00   1.211   +93.7%   22.4%      4.18
// Better on train, test AND full period, on both return and drawdown, and
// graded smoothly across scales rather than spiking at one.
// Banking BOTH sides on the same grid drops net to +29-41%: the asymmetry
// is the mechanism, not the grid.
// Stacking with Daye's Q4 was inside noise (PF 1.265 vs 1.261), so the
// simpler single rule ships and Q4 is left as an off-by-default option.
//
// ── STAYING PROFITABLE WHEN GOLD FALLS ──────────────────────────────
// Long-only makes the most money over 6.7 years (+171.9%, PF 1.480, DD
// 11.8%) but loses in falling markets (-2.8% Feb-Jul 2026). Symmetric
// long+short survives the fall but costs 61 points of return.
//
// The fix is that price BELOW the EMA and the EMA FALLING are very
// different states. Price dips below a flat EMA constantly during
// uptrend pullbacks, and shorts taken there are what bleed the
// long-only edge away. Requiring the EMA to be below its own value 200
// bars ago makes shorts nearly silent in bull markets and active in
// bear ones. With the counter-trend side also sized at 0.75x:
//
//    config                       full net    PF     DD    falling window
//    A long-only                   +171.9%  1.480  11.8%      -2.8%
//    B both, symmetric             +110.6%  1.261  19.0%      +4.6%
//    C slope-gated shorts @0.75x   +160.1%  1.386  12.8%      +5.0%   <- shipped
//
// C keeps 93% of long-only's return AND is profitable in the fall.
// Raising the short multiplier to 1.5 buys more downside (+10.5% in the
// falling window) for less total return (+145.9%).
//
// GATES on the held-out block (n=362, PF 1.525, +64.0%, DD 8.9%):
//    random null at matched n: 88.8th percentile     FAIL (needs 95th)
//    deflated Sharpe 0.577 at 50 trials               FAIL
//    return/drawdown 7.19 vs buy-and-hold 3.44        PASS
// So the entry still is not demonstrably better than random. What IS
// demonstrable is the risk profile: 64% of buy-and-hold's return for
// less than a third of its drawdown, and positive in both regimes.
//
// SHIPPED CONFIG: both directions, trail 6 ATR, SMA 750, EMA 2000 gate,
// shorts slope-gated at 200 bars and sized 0.75x, JEAFX 2.50 grid
// banking 50% of shorts.
//    train 2019-23   PF 1.250  +38.6%
//    test  2023-26   PF 1.525  +64.0%  DD  8.9%
//    full 6.7 years  PF 1.386 +160.1%  DD 12.8%   n=922
//    Feb-Jul 2026    PF 1.454   +5.0%           (gold -15.8%)
//
// ── SETTINGS ARE TIMEFRAME-SPECIFIC. USE THE RIGHT COLUMN. ──────────
// Every lookback here is in BARS, so moving timeframe silently doubles or
// halves it in real time. SMA 750 is 7.8 days on 15m and 15.6 days on 30m.
// Running the 15m numbers on a 30m chart is a different strategy.
//
//   input             15m        30m        what it is on both
//   Breakout          50         25         ~12 hours
//   Trail (x ATR)     6.0        4.0        ATR is larger on 30m
//   Trend SMA         750        375        ~8 days
//   Regime EMA        2000       1000       ~21 days
//   Slope lookback    200        100        ~2 days
//   Key-level grid    2.50       3.75       ~1x ATR
//   Short risk        0.75       0.75
//
// Measured over the full 6.7 years with each column on its own timeframe:
//   15m: PF 1.386  +160.1%  DD 12.8%  n=922
//   30m: PF 1.404  +174.8%  DD  9.0%  n=888   <- better on every metric
// The 30m column also stays positive in the falling window (+4.4%) and
// returns +32.4% over Jan 2025 - Jul 2026.
//
// IF YOUR RESULTS DO NOT MATCH, CHECK THIS FIRST: TradingView KEEPS the
// inputs you previously set when a script is updated. A chart that has
// had settings changed will NOT pick up new defaults. Open settings and
// hit "Defaults -> Reset settings", then set the column above. A trade
// count noticeably HIGHER than expected means a filter is switched off -
// usually the slope gate or the EMA gate.
//
// ── REPRODUCING THESE NUMBERS ON TRADINGVIEW ────────────────────────
// The +160.1% figure comes from 157,366 bars of 15m data (6.7 years).
// **NO TRADINGVIEW PLAN WILL LOAD THAT ON A 15m CHART** - the intraday
// limit is roughly 5,000 bars (free/Pro) to 20,000 (Premium). So that
// number is not reproducible here, and any attempt to match it will
// fail for reasons that have nothing to do with this code.
//
// What you SHOULD see, by bar budget (most recent bars):
//    bars      window from    n     WR      PF      net      DD
//    5,000     2026-05-13    21   47.6%   1.521    +2.4%    2.8%
//   10,000     2026-02-25    43   46.5%   1.365    +3.5%    3.0%
//   20,000     2025-09-23    91   45.1%   1.768   +17.3%    4.0%
//   40,000     2024-11-15   215   39.5%   1.603   +40.1%    8.6%
//
// The first ~2,200 bars of ANY window are warm-up (EMA 2000 + 200-bar
// slope), so a 5,000-bar chart only really trades its last ~2,800 bars
// and will show a handful of trades. Judge it on the largest window your
// plan allows, and remember n=21 tells you nothing.
//
// TWO THINGS THAT WILL DESYNC IT FROM THE RESEARCH
//  1. slippage is set in TICKS, not points. This assumes mintick 0.01,
//     so slippage=5 means $0.05. If your feed quotes gold to 1 decimal
//     (mintick 0.1) then 5 ticks is $0.50 - ten times the modelled cost,
//     and results will look far worse. Check the symbol's mintick and
//     scale this input to match.
//  2. Feed. The research uses London Strategic Edge spot; TradingView
//     here is OANDA. A matched-window check came back within 2 trades
//     and 0.017 PF, so the feeds agree closely - but not to the cent.
//
// HONEST LIMITS
//  - One instrument, one timeframe. Untested on anything else.
//  - The test window is a historic gold bull run. A long-biased trend
//    system will underperform badly in a range or a bear market. That
//    is not a flaw to tune away, it is what this design is.
//  - Higher trail settings show higher PF on far fewer trades. PF 4.15
//    appears at n=54 -- and random entries score 4.175 there. Do not
//    chase the headline number; n>=250 is where the estimate is stable.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//@version=6
strategy("Gold Trend — Trailing Stop",
     overlay                 = true,
     max_lines_count         = 500,
     max_labels_count        = 500,
     initial_capital         = 10000,
     currency                = currency.USD,
     default_qty_type        = strategy.cash,
     default_qty_value       = 1000,
     commission_type         = strategy.commission.cash_per_contract,
     commission_value        = 0.07,
     slippage                = 5,
     process_orders_on_close = true,
     margin_long             = 5,
     margin_short            = 5,
     calc_on_every_tick      = false,
     // EMA 2000 plus a 200-bar slope lookback means this script reaches
     // ~2,200 bars back. Pine's default historical buffer is 300, which
     // throws "requested historical offset beyond the buffer" or silently
     // yields na. This is the single most likely cause of a Pine result
     // not matching the research.
     max_bars_back           = 5000)

// ── ⓪ Auto-scale ────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every lookback below is in BARS, so changing timeframe silently doubles
// or halves it in real time. That has now caused two failed reproductions,
// so the script derives its own settings from the chart instead. The
// formulas were checked against the two hand-tuned columns and land on
// them almost exactly:
//    15m -> entry 48, SMA 768, EMA 2016, slope 192, trail 6.00, grid 2.50
//    30m -> entry 24, SMA 384, EMA 1008, slope  96, trail 4.24, grid 3.54
//    (hand-tuned were 50/750/2000/200/6.0/2.50 and 25/375/1000/100/4.0/3.75)
// Turn it off only to reproduce a specific historical run.
bool  autoScale = input.bool(true, "Auto-scale all settings to the chart timeframe", group="⓪ Auto-scale",
     tooltip="ON is strongly recommended. With it on, the inputs below are IGNORED and derived from the timeframe, so moving between 15m and 30m cannot silently change the strategy. The dashboard shows the values actually in use.")

// ── ① Core (these three are the whole system) ────────────────────
int   entryLen  = input.int(50, "Breakout lookback (bars)", minval=5, group="① Core",
     tooltip="Long when close exceeds the highest high of the prior N bars. Validated at 50 and 100; both work. This is a timing trigger, not an edge.")
float trailMult = input.float(6.0, "Trailing stop (x ATR)", minval=1.0, step=0.5, group="① Core",
     tooltip="THE key parameter. Validated: 4.0 -> PF 1.40, 6.0 -> 1.60, 8.0 -> 1.59, 14.0 -> 1.75 out of sample. Below 4 the system loses. Wider = fewer trades, higher PF, but a less reliable estimate.")
bool  longOnly  = input.bool(false, "Long only (leave OFF — both directions is the shipped config)", group="① Core",
     tooltip="THIS IS A REGIME BET, NOT A SETTING. Long-only wins every rising year and loses in falling ones. Long+short is the reverse. See the year table in the header. Gold is down over the last six months, so shorts ON is currently the better side. Reassess if that changes.")
int   atrLen    = input.int(14, "ATR length", minval=2, group="① Core")

// ── ② Risk ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
float riskPct   = input.float(1.0, "Risk per trade (%)", step=0.25, group="② Risk")
float maxLev    = input.float(20, "Max leverage (notional cap)", minval=1, group="② Risk",
     tooltip="BUG-012 guard. Without a notional cap, risk-based sizing silently requests more than buying power and TradingView skips the order with no error.")
bool  useSma    = input.bool(true, "Trend filter: only long above the SMA, short below", group="② Risk",
     tooltip="Improves every window measured, but does NOT pass a significance test - see the header. Harmless to run, unwise to believe in.")
int   smaLen    = input.int(750, "Trend SMA length (bars)", minval=50, group="② Risk",
     tooltip="750 bars on 15m is about 8 days. Selected on the TRAINING half only.")
bool  useSma2   = input.bool(true, "SECOND trend filter: the slow SMA must agree too", group="② Risk",
     tooltip="The confluence layer. The fast SMA (750, ~8 days) and the slow one (2000, ~3 weeks) must BOTH be on the trade's side, on top of the EMA regime gate. Measured on 157,366 bars: full-period PF 1.379 -> 1.421, net +155.9% -> +157.7%, and drawdown 12.30% -> 9.63%. Return-per-drawdown goes 12.68 -> 16.36, which is the number that actually matters. Both halves agree: train PF 1.289 -> 1.335 with drawdown 12.3% -> 9.6%, out of sample 1.610 -> 1.624. Requiring only ONE of the two is much worse (train PF 1.149) - it is the agreement that does the work, not either average on its own.")
int   sma2Len   = input.int(2000, "Slow SMA length (bars)", minval=100, group="② Risk",
     tooltip="2000 bars on 15m is about 3 weeks - the same horizon as the regime EMA, but an SMA rather than an EMA, so it turns later and disagrees at exactly the moments that matter.")
bool  smaExit   = input.bool(false, "Also exit when price crosses back through the SMA", group="② Risk",
     tooltip="Tested and rejected: it cuts winners early. Train PF 1.030 vs 1.048 with the gate alone.")
bool  useSlope  = input.bool(true, "Shorts only when the EMA is FALLING (not merely below it)", group="② Risk",
     tooltip="This is what makes the system profitable in a falling market without giving up the trend returns. Price sits below a FLAT EMA constantly during uptrend pullbacks; the EMA actually declining is a much rarer state and is what a bear regime looks like. Gating shorts on slope keeps them nearly silent in bull markets, where counter-trend shorts are what bleed away the long-only edge.")
int   slopeLen  = input.int(200, "Slope lookback (bars)", minval=20, group="② Risk",
     tooltip="EMA must be below its own value this many bars ago. 200 bars = ~2 days on 15m. Tested 50/100/200/300; 200 gave the best full-period return.")
float shortRisk = input.float(0.75, "Short risk multiplier", minval=0.1, maxval=3.0, step=0.05, group="② Risk",
     tooltip="Counter-trend trades sized smaller. 0.75 gave the best full-period result (+160.1%); raise toward 1.5 for more downside protection at the cost of total return (+145.9% but +10.5% in the falling window).")
bool  useRegime = input.bool(true, "EMA gate: only long above it, short below", group="② Risk",
     tooltip="EMA 2000 (~3 weeks on 15m). Combined with the SMA750 filter it lifts full-period PF to 1.202 and cuts drawdown.")
int   regimeLen = input.int(2000, "Regime EMA length", minval=20, group="② Risk")

// ── ④ Key-level exit / reversal ─────────────────────────────────
bool  useKL     = input.bool(false, "Exit at the next key level", group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="DEFAULTED OFF ON 2026-07-31 AFTER A LIVE RESULT CONTRADICTED THE RESEARCH. On Feb 2 - Jul 31 2026 this build returned PF 0.702 and -2.99% on TradingView while the Python engine had the same window at PF 1.385 and +3.54%. The fingerprint is in the trade stats: largest profit 92.94 against largest loss 120.38. In a system with NO profit target the biggest winner must dwarf the biggest loser, and here it does not - something is cutting the winners. Python on the same window has largest profit 239.48 against largest loss 95.00, which is the correct shape. The level exit is the newest thing in the script and it is the only component whose job is to cut a winner short, so it is off until the two agree. Research says turning it off costs almost nothing anyway: +3.54% -> +3.47% on that window.")
string klSource = input.string("Chart key levels", "Which levels", options=["Chart key levels", "Quarter grid"], group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="CHART KEY LEVELS reads klPrices[] — the exact prices the embedded SpacemanBTC module is drawing on this chart (PDH/PDL, PWH/PWL, PMH/PML, opens, session ranges, whichever you have enabled in the KL groups below). QUARTER GRID is the old synthetic .00/.25/.50/.75 grid. Measured back to back they are within noise of each other: train PF 1.289 (chart levels) vs 1.297 (grid), out of sample 1.610 vs 1.598. Chart levels are the default because they are the ones you can actually see and mark up.")
float klGrid    = input.float(2.50, "Quarter-grid spacing (points)", minval=0.25, step=0.25, group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="Only used when 'Which levels' is set to Quarter grid. JEAFX quarters of gold's 10-unit handles: 4000.00 / 4002.50 / 4005.00 / 4007.50.")
float klMinAtr  = input.float(3.0, "Target must be at least (x ATR from entry)", minval=0.0, step=0.5, group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="WITHOUT THIS THE TARGET IS NOT A TARGET. The module draws 36 levels, and on this chart the median one sits 0.46 ATR ahead of price while the stop sits 6 ATR behind it. Banking 75% there risks 6 to make 0.46 - a losing structure no matter how often it wins, which is exactly the 56.9% win rate and 0.702 profit factor the live run produced. This forces the script to skip past levels that are too close and take the first one that is a real distance away. Set it to 0 to reproduce the old behaviour.")
float klTol     = input.float(0.25, "Reached-level tolerance (points)", minval=0.05, step=0.05, group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="A level counts as reached within this distance. Swept 0.05-0.50 ATR: PF moves 1.272-1.296 on train and 1.612-1.598 out of sample. Flat, so nothing here is knife-edge.")
bool  useKLEntry= input.bool(false, "Also require a key level NEAR the entry", group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="Adds the level as an ENTRY confluence too: the breakout only counts if a drawn level sits within the distance below. MEASURED, and it costs money: full period net +157.7% -> +120.9% and PF 1.421 -> 1.385, with the same effect at 0.5 and 2.0 ATR. Level touches carry no directional information (see the header), so this filter removes trades without improving their quality. Off by default; here because it is the natural thing to want to try.")
float klEntryAtr= input.float(1.0, "Level must be within (x ATR of entry)", minval=0.1, step=0.1, group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal")
float klTake    = input.float(75, "% closed at the level", minval=10, maxval=100, step=5, group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="75 measured best on both halves (train PF 1.289, OOS 1.610) but 60-90 are all within noise. The remainder keeps running on the trail — that is what preserves the big trend winners.")
bool  klFlip    = input.bool(false, "REVERSE at the level (close and take the opposite side)", group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="Turns the level into a stop-and-reverse: the level closes the whole position and opens the other way on the next bar, still subject to the regime filters. MEASURED, and it does not pay. On both sides it is the worst config tested: train PF 0.932 and net -14.8% vs 1.244/+85.6% for banking. Restricted to the counter-trend side it is merely flat: train PF 1.244 vs 1.289 for a 75% bank, OOS 1.588 vs 1.610. The toggle is here because you asked for it and it is honest to let you see it, not because the data supports leaving it on.")
bool  klShorts  = input.bool(true, "SHORTS ONLY — let longs run (this is what makes it work)", group="④ Key-Level Exit / Reversal",
     tooltip="CRITICAL, and now confirmed a third time on the real chart levels. Exiting BOTH sides at key levels collapses the system: full-period PF 1.023 and net +5.9% against 1.333/+152.5% for doing nothing at all. Restricted to the counter-trend side it is the best config measured: PF 1.379, net +155.9%, drawdown 12.3% vs the baseline's 13.6%. A level exit removes exactly the size that would have captured a large move, so only take it where there is no large move to catch.")

// ── ⑤ Daye Q4 time-based take (alternative, OFF by default) ─────
bool  useQ4     = input.bool(false, "Bank in Daye's Q4 instead (time-based)", group="⑤ Daye Q4 (alternative)",
     tooltip="Daye's daily quarters in ET: Q1 Asia 18-00 range build, Q2 London 00-06 expansion, Q3 NY AM 06-12 continuation, Q4 NY PM 12-18 REVERSAL / PROFIT-TAKING. Q4 is where the source says to bank.")
float q4Take    = input.float(50, "% banked at the Q4 open", minval=10, maxval=100, step=5, group="⑤ Daye Q4 (alternative)")
bool  q4Shorts  = input.bool(true, "SHORTS ONLY", group="⑤ Daye Q4 (alternative)",
     tooltip="CRITICAL. Banking symmetrically DESTROYS the system: taking 50% off both sides drops full-period net from +61.5% to +21.4%. Banking only the counter-trend side improves every window tested. A take removes exactly the size that captures the big move -- so only take it where there is no big move to catch.")
int   q4StartUtc= input.int(17, "Q4 start hour (UTC)", minval=0, maxval=23, group="⑤ Daye Q4 (alternative)",
     tooltip="12:00 ET = 17:00 UTC. Adjust for DST if you want to be exact.")

// ── ③ Display ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
bool  showStop  = input.bool(true, "Plot the trailing stop", group="③ Display")
bool  showBand  = input.bool(true, "Plot the breakout level", group="③ Display")
bool  showDash  = input.bool(true, "Show dashboard", group="③ Display")

// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// EFFECTIVE SETTINGS — derived from the timeframe when auto-scale is on.
// Targets: breakout ~12h, SMA ~8 days, EMA ~21 days, slope ~2 days.
// Trail and grid scale with the square root of the timeframe because ATR
// grows roughly as sqrt(time).
// ═════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
float tfMin      = timeframe.in_seconds() / 60.0
int   entryLenE  = autoScale ? math.max(5,  int(12.0 * 60.0 / tfMin))       : entryLen
int   smaLenE    = autoScale ? math.max(50, int(8.0 * 24.0 * 60.0 / tfMin)) : smaLen
int   regimeLenE = autoScale ? math.max(20, int(21.0 * 24.0 * 60.0 / tfMin)): regimeLen
int   slopeLenE  = autoScale ? math.max(20, int(2.0 * 24.0 * 60.0 / tfMin)) : slopeLen
int   sma2LenE   = autoScale ? math.max(100, int(21.0 * 24.0 * 60.0 / tfMin)): sma2Len
float trailMultE = autoScale ? 6.0 * math.sqrt(15.0 / tfMin)               : trailMult
float klGridE    = autoScale ? 2.50 * math.sqrt(tfMin / 15.0)              : klGrid
float klTolE     = autoScale ? klGridE * 0.10                              : klTol

// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// EMBEDDED: SpacemanBTC IDWM Key Levels V13.1 (verbatim port)
// Standing user directive: every strategy in this repo embeds the
// key-level indicator and renders it exactly as the standalone does.
// Canonical copy + port deltas: trader_playbooks/skills/key_levels_module.pine
// NO LONGER DISPLAY ONLY. The module exports every level it draws into
// klPrices[], and the trade logic below closes (or reverses) at the
// nearest one ahead of the position. The number the strategy acts on is
// literally the line you can see. That is why the whole strategy block
// now sits BELOW this module instead of above it.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// ── KL inputs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
displayStyle  = input.string(defval='Standard', title='Display Style', options=['Standard', 'Right Anchored'], inline='Display', group='KL Display')
mergebool     = input.bool(defval=true, title='Merge Levels?', inline='Display', group='KL Display')
distanceright = input.int(defval=30, title='Distance', minval=5, maxval=500, inline='Dist', group='KL Display')
radistance    = input.int(defval=250, title='Anchor Distance', minval=5, maxval=500, inline='Dist', group='KL Display')
labelsize     = input.string(defval='Medium', title='Text Size', options=['Small', 'Medium', 'Large'], group='KL Display')
linesize      = input.string(defval='Small', title='Line Width', options=['Small', 'Medium', 'Large'], inline='Line', group='KL Display')
linestyle     = input.string(defval='Solid', title='Line Style', options=['Solid', 'Dashed', 'Dotted'], inline='Line', group='KL Display')

GlobalTextType    = input.bool(defval=false, title='Global Text ShortHand', tooltip='Enable for shorthand text on all text', group='KL Display')
var globalcoloring = input.bool(defval=false, title='Global Coloring', tooltip='Enable for all color controls via one color', inline='GC', group='KL Display')
